# Includes the /api/v1 suffix, matching mandana-web's convention — see
# lib/api/server-client.ts's StripApiV1 type trick for why.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://d3n6d6boq936jc.cloudfront.net/api/v1

# Same value as NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL above -- deliberately duplicated,
# not aliased. NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars are inlined into the JS bundle at `next
# build` time (in server code too, not just the client bundle), so on a
# host like Amplify, setting/changing NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL in the
# console has no effect until the next rebuild. notification-bell.tsx's
# streamBaseUrl() reads THIS (non-public) var first, at real request time,
# specifically so the notification bell's realtime stream can be
# reconfigured with a redeploy rather than a full rebuild. Falls back to
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL if unset, so this is optional in dev.
API_BASE_URL=https://d3n6d6boq936jc.cloudfront.net/api/v1


# Overrides the auto-detected cookie `secure` attribute (see
# lib/auth/cookies.ts#isSecureRequest). Leave unset in normal dev/prod — it
# is derived from x-forwarded-proto / Origin / request URL scheme. Only set
# this if a TLS-terminating proxy in front of this app does NOT forward
# x-forwarded-proto, so the auto-detection can't see that traffic is HTTPS.
# AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=true

# Next.js encrypts Server Action payloads/IDs with a key derived per-build
# by default, which is fine for a single instance but differs between
# processes. REQUIRED the moment this app runs as more than one instance
# (multiple containers/Lambdas) -- which Amplify's SSR hosting always is --
# otherwise an action invoked against one instance can fail to decrypt on
# another. Concretely for this app: mintStreamTicketAction() (the
# notification bell's EventSource auth) is a Server Action, so a missing
# key here can silently break realtime notifications on Amplify even when
# API_BASE_URL/NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL are both set correctly -- watch for
# the Server Action's own request in the Network tab. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
